  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Laeticutis cristata is a small, delicate polypore fungus belonging to the Albatrellaceae family, characterized by its distinctive crested or ridged cap surface. This woodland fungus plays an important ecological role as a decomposer of decaying wood in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Crested Cap Surface",
      "description": "The most distinctive feature is the cap's prominent radial ridges or crests that give the fungus its 'cristata' name, appearing almost brain-like or folded.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface",
      "description": "The underside features small, angular pores that are white to pale tan and may run down the stalk, typical of Albatrellaceae members.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size and Fragility",
      "description": "Generally small fruitbodies (1-3 cm wide) with a delicate, brittle texture that distinguishes it from larger polypore species.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Laeticutis cristata grows on decaying hardwood logs and woody debris in moist forest floors throughout New England's deciduous and mixed woodlands.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The genus name 'Laeticutis' refers to the smooth cuticle of the cap, while 'cristata' means crested, perfectly describing its unique sculptured appearance that resembles miniature ridged artwork.",
